a national championship
a couple years prior.

00:17:51.367 --> 00:17:53.588
- Was that a part of
you doing shit like hey,

00:17:53.588 --> 00:17:54.920
it might be cool to be on the East Coast,

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.348
I've always been a West Coast kid?

00:17:56.348 --> 00:17:58.417
- And it was the Duke and UNC rivalry.

00:17:58.417 --> 00:17:59.733
- You wanted to get into that?

00:17:59.733 --> 00:18:02.775
- Yeah, the ACC was
unbelievable, but you know,

00:18:02.775 --> 00:18:05.423
UCLA had been to two straight
Final Fours at the time.

00:18:05.423 --> 00:18:08.804
Ben Howland was very
good at teaching his guys

00:18:08.804 --> 00:18:12.136
and having his guys go to the
next level and have success

00:18:12.136 --> 00:18:13.535
and I think that continued.

00:18:13.535 --> 00:18:17.416
- Remind me, remind me where
we were in the high school,

00:18:17.416 --> 00:18:19.346
coming straight out of
high school versus--

00:18:19.346 --> 00:18:20.757
- Two years removed.

00:18:20.757 --> 00:18:23.878
So I think what, 2005 was the last year?

00:18:23.878 --> 00:18:25.128
- [Gary] So you had to go to school?

00:18:25.128 --> 00:18:26.459
- Had to go, but I probably
would have gone out.

00:18:26.459 --> 00:18:27.292
- You would have?

00:18:27.292 --> 00:18:28.125
- [Kevin] Yes.

00:18:28.125 --> 00:18:28.958
- Flat out?

00:18:28.958 --> 00:18:29.791
- [Kevin] Flat out.

00:18:29.791 --> 00:18:32.083
- Did you fundamentally know
you're gonna be one and done?

00:18:32.083 --> 00:18:34.346
- [Kevin] Yes, always knew.
- From day one?

00:18:34.346 --> 00:18:35.654
- Yeah, it was no secret.

00:18:35.654 --> 00:18:38.862
So they came down to
UCLA and North Carolina

00:18:38.862 --> 00:18:41.631
and finally Kerry Keating
whose assistant coach there

00:18:41.631 --> 00:18:46.054
at the time, still heavily
involved in California,

00:18:46.054 --> 00:18:47.943
not only athletics but basketball as well,

00:18:47.943 --> 00:18:49.383
he just said you know are we doing this?

00:18:49.383 --> 00:18:50.783
I remember I was at a tournament in Vegas

00:18:50.783 --> 00:18:52.184
where they have the 500 teams--

00:18:52.184 --> 00:18:53.017
- He just went in for the kill?

00:18:53.017 --> 00:18:54.439
- He played five games

00:18:54.439 --> 00:18:56.080
on fucking Sunday to win.
- He found the right moment?

00:18:56.080 --> 00:18:57.160
- Yeah he found the right moment.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:18:59.158
I was like okay, you got me.

00:18:59.158 --> 00:19:01.096
Committed to UCLA, ended up going.

00:19:01.096 --> 00:19:04.399
That was my rising, senior
year end up going to UCLA

00:19:04.399 --> 00:19:07.571
and made best friends
with Russell Westbrook

00:19:07.571 --> 00:19:09.420
and we just talked about how we were

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.040
going to the NBA every single day.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:11.931
- [Gary] That's so cool.

00:19:11.931 --> 00:19:12.802
- Yeah.

00:19:12.802 --> 00:19:14.160
- And you guys did and you did well.

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:15.379
- [Kevin] And we did, and
we we're four and five pick.

00:19:15.379 --> 00:19:16.212
pretty cool.

00:19:16.212 --> 00:19:17.045
- Very, very cool.

00:19:17.045 --> 00:19:17.878
- [Kevin] We were roommates.

00:19:17.878 --> 00:19:19.923
- It's real cool, real cool.

00:19:19.923 --> 00:19:24.271
So what's the you know, just
there's a random question,

00:19:24.271 --> 00:19:25.403
'cause I really want to get into some of

00:19:25.403 --> 00:19:26.741
the off the court stuff.

00:19:26.741 --> 00:19:28.143
What was the biggest surprise?

00:19:28.143 --> 00:19:29.970
You had a dad that was in league,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:32.971
you were groomed for this,
you know the pedigree,

00:19:32.971 --> 00:19:35.480
it's fucking 24/7, even when it wasn't,

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:37.242
it's running in the background.

00:19:37.242 --> 00:19:39.590
What was the biggest surprise?

00:19:39.590 --> 00:19:43.423
- There is nothing you
can do to prepare for--

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:45.793
- [Gary] The speed?

00:19:45.793 --> 00:19:46.980
- The speed, the grind,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:50.897
but playing in Texas
against Dirk or Tim Duncan

00:19:53.101 --> 00:19:57.052
flying back home on a
back-to-back in Minnesota

00:19:57.052 --> 00:19:59.740
and playing KG in the Boston Celtics.

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:01.618
There's just nothing you can do.

00:20:01.618 --> 00:20:03.860
You're playing two
games a week in college.

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:05.669
In the tournament you're
playing Thursday, Saturday,

00:20:05.669 --> 00:20:07.562
Thursday, Sunday, and then
you wait to the next week

00:20:07.562 --> 00:20:10.943
and then you go to the Sweet
16, Elite Eight, Final Four.

00:20:10.943 --> 00:20:12.393
- Right, so not only is the volume

00:20:12.393 --> 00:20:14.391
and speed of which the games come at you,

00:20:14.391 --> 00:20:15.270
you're also playing against

00:20:15.270 --> 00:20:16.516
the best fucking players in the world.

00:20:16.516 --> 00:20:18.448
- And we had so, I mean,
we had lottery picks,

00:20:18.448 --> 00:20:20.509
we had guys that were first rounders,

00:20:20.509 --> 00:20:22.169
guys that have huge success in the league,

00:20:22.169 --> 00:20:24.486
in the Pac-10 at the time, Pac-12 now,

00:20:24.486 --> 00:20:26.928
but there's nothing you
can do to emulate that.

00:20:26.928 --> 00:20:29.780
I played Dirk and yeah, Tim Duncan

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:31.609
within the first five games of my career.

00:20:31.609 --> 00:20:33.408
Tony Parker put up 55 points on us.

00:20:33.408 --> 00:20:34.691
(laughing)

00:20:34.691 --> 00:20:37.039
I'm like this is the NBA?

00:20:37.039 --> 00:20:38.862
So I got blindsided.

00:20:38.862 --> 00:20:40.627
- Right, 'cause meanwhile
you're like five minutes away

00:20:40.627 --> 00:20:41.979
from playing a video game with Tony Parker

00:20:41.979 --> 00:20:43.340
and like he's not that fast.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:44.688
- 100%, yeah exactly.

00:20:44.688 --> 00:20:47.567
And that's what we did
is we played video games

00:20:47.567 --> 00:20:49.769
all the time in our locker room at UCLA

00:20:49.769 --> 00:20:53.319
and we're like I'm a pick Bron and okay,

00:20:53.319 --> 00:20:54.644
I'm a pick the Lakers.

00:20:54.644 --> 00:20:55.477
I'm gonna be Kobe.

00:20:55.477 --> 00:20:57.502
Then you get there and it's like okay,

00:20:57.502 --> 00:21:00.614
Kobe's putting 40 on us every single time

00:21:00.614 --> 00:21:01.447
and there's nothing you can do.

00:21:01.447 --> 00:21:03.113
He'll tell you right
straight to your face,

00:21:03.113 --> 00:21:04.473
he goes, "You guys don't
have a chance tonight.

00:21:04.473 --> 00:21:06.113
"It's Friday night and I'm the show."

00:21:06.113 --> 00:21:09.895
- "Who's the one player
that you like warm,

00:21:09.895 --> 00:21:11.596
you know tuck it in, you're checking in,

00:21:11.596 --> 00:21:14.028
you get on the court and
then you actually see him

00:21:14.028 --> 00:21:16.617
and it was like actually the
biggest response you had,

00:21:16.617 --> 00:21:18.738
whether it was something
funny, an inside joke,

00:21:18.738 --> 00:21:20.428
because you remember being at a camp

00:21:20.428 --> 00:21:22.027
or whether it was Kobe
and you're like fuck,

00:21:22.027 --> 00:21:24.248
this is actually Kobe.

00:21:24.248 --> 00:21:27.086
- It's Kobe, 100% Kobe.

00:21:27.086 --> 00:21:29.297
'Cause there's just I mean I've,

00:21:29.297 --> 00:21:30.737
and this is no disrespect to Bron

00:21:30.737 --> 00:21:32.546
because he brings them out too

00:21:32.546 --> 00:21:34.057
and there's that energy in the gym

00:21:34.057 --> 00:21:35.579
and you're getting everybody's best game.

00:21:35.579 --> 00:21:37.248
Your whole mindset shifts too

00:21:37.248 --> 00:21:39.837
when you get on a team like that.

00:21:39.837 --> 00:21:41.239
I mean we were a super team

00:21:41.239 --> 00:21:43.027
and we were a team that
was expected go the finals,

00:21:43.027 --> 00:21:45.544
expected to have the potential
to win a championship.

00:21:45.544 --> 00:21:48.739
But when I first got into the league,

00:21:48.739 --> 00:21:53.239
Kobe Bryant, it didn't matter
if it was a soft Tuesday

00:21:54.199 --> 00:21:57.865
in Minneapolis, Kobe's bringing 'em out.

00:21:57.865 --> 00:21:59.306
I mean there's a there's a buzz

00:21:59.306 --> 00:22:00.677
in the city that Kobe is there.

00:22:00.677 --> 00:22:02.067
- Yeah and like the worst part,

00:22:02.067 --> 00:22:03.707
which I still want to punch every person

00:22:03.707 --> 00:22:05.318
in the building when I'm there,

00:22:05.318 --> 00:22:08.238
like Kobe drops the first
bucket, it's two nothing,

00:22:08.238 --> 00:22:12.177
Lakers, Minnesota, and 73%
of the audience is cheering.

00:22:12.177 --> 00:22:13.938
- Oh no, it was and by the way,

00:22:13.938 --> 00:22:17.185
it's a Minneapolis Lakers,
it's a home game for 'em,

00:22:17.185 --> 00:22:20.007
but I mean it's like
everywhere they went--

00:22:20.007 --> 00:22:21.198
- This is why it's great
that I didn't end up

00:22:21.198 --> 00:22:22.498
being a fucking professional athlete

00:22:22.498 --> 00:22:24.808
because like at two nothing
when Kobe gets that jump shot

00:22:24.808 --> 00:22:27.029
I'm like running back down the court

00:22:27.029 --> 00:22:30.308
like trying to argue with
fans like fuck you, leave.

00:22:30.308 --> 00:22:32.266
I hate visiting fans.

00:22:32.266 --> 00:22:34.198
When I go to visiting games as a Jets fan,

00:22:34.198 --> 00:22:35.797
I don't say a word.

00:22:35.797 --> 00:22:37.304
- I'm trying to think of who you would,

00:22:37.304 --> 00:22:39.277
what's like looking in a mirror for you

00:22:39.277 --> 00:22:40.238
in this league today?

00:22:40.238 --> 00:22:41.172
- Like Ron Artest meets,

00:22:41.172 --> 00:22:42.406
like whatever the worst things

00:22:42.406 --> 00:22:45.005
that have ever happened in sports, that.

00:22:45.005 --> 00:22:47.324
Because I'm willing like it gets--

00:22:47.324 --> 00:22:48.495
- So you are like a,

00:22:48.495 --> 00:22:50.315
speaking of Randy Johnson
in the Seattle Mariner,

00:22:50.315 --> 00:22:51.395
you were like a Lou Piniella,

00:22:51.395 --> 00:22:53.775
you take out the base
and you throw the shit.

00:22:53.775 --> 00:22:56.405
- What's crazy is not in life,

00:22:56.405 --> 00:22:58.904
in business people like steal
a million dollars from me,

00:22:58.904 --> 00:23:01.586
I'm like phew and that's
why I have to be careful

00:23:01.586 --> 00:23:03.646
to not project, maybe what I hate about

00:23:03.646 --> 00:23:06.095
being a fan is I'm not in control.

00:23:06.095 --> 00:23:08.354
Like when I'm in control,

00:23:08.354 --> 00:23:11.576
like the CEO of this company,
everything bad, easy.

00:23:11.576 --> 00:23:13.885
Maybe I'm just frustrated
as a fan like fuck,

00:23:13.885 --> 00:23:15.637
like you know like, right?

00:23:15.637 --> 00:23:16.774
Like maybe that's what it is,

00:23:16.774 --> 00:23:18.412
so maybe I actually am
completely full of shit

00:23:18.412 --> 00:23:20.165
and it's completely the other way

00:23:20.165 --> 00:23:21.984
when I would actually be
on the corridor field.

00:23:21.984 --> 00:23:24.675
But no, 'cause when we
play I'm like crazy.

00:23:24.675 --> 00:23:26.356
- So speaking out of control though--

00:23:26.356 --> 00:23:27.566
- [Man] You elbow us,

00:23:27.566 --> 00:23:29.186
you put with like your foot underneath.

00:23:29.186 --> 00:23:30.019
- Yeah, like a step.

00:23:30.019 --> 00:23:33.026
I don't, like when you're
not, like here's my thought.

00:23:33.026 --> 00:23:34.085
When you're not--

00:23:34.085 --> 00:23:35.204
- [Kevin] And he's been
begging me this whole time

00:23:35.204 --> 00:23:36.395
to come out and play some ball like--

00:23:36.395 --> 00:23:38.143
- I'm always scared
because while you're still,

00:23:38.143 --> 00:23:39.494
when you retire it will be easier,

00:23:39.494 --> 00:23:40.733
'cause when you're active I'm like fuck,

00:23:40.733 --> 00:23:43.212
what if I get weird and
like then it's like on

00:23:43.212 --> 00:23:45.023
and then people actually
found out the real story,

00:23:45.023 --> 00:23:46.073
it would be like really fucked up.

00:23:46.073 --> 00:23:48.235
Like I'm always, like I
invite Jet players over

00:23:48.235 --> 00:23:49.854
and we hang and I'm like fuck,

00:23:49.854 --> 00:23:51.337
I better be careful here
'cause I actually care.

00:23:51.337 --> 00:23:54.756
- I'm gonna be taking HGH and
testosterone at that time too.

00:23:54.756 --> 00:23:58.320
So I'm gonna have rage
fits and just go after you.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:23:59.153
- Let's jump to it

00:23:59.153 --> 00:24:00.931
'cause I don't want it
to get away from us.

00:24:00.931 --> 00:24:02.259
Let's talk about some of the things

00:24:02.259 --> 00:24:03.532
you've been talking about.

00:24:03.532 --> 00:24:04.469
Some of things you're passionate about

00:24:04.469 --> 00:24:06.741
because I think it's
uncomfortably important

00:24:06.741 --> 00:24:09.052
and I think you're building momentum.

00:24:09.052 --> 00:24:11.673
Just like I think about so many people

00:24:11.673 --> 00:24:12.962
that have brought so much value,

00:24:12.962 --> 00:24:15.411
whether it's Curt Flood
or Jackie Robinson,

00:24:15.411 --> 00:24:18.268
with color barriers or
just all these things.

00:24:18.268 --> 00:24:21.184
You know watching some of stuff
Kyle Korver's talking about.

00:24:21.184 --> 00:24:23.663
I'm really excited
about what I believe you

00:24:23.663 --> 00:24:25.985
and a couple others have started
the momentum conversation.

00:24:25.985 --> 00:24:28.365
For a lot of people that don't
even know where I'm going,

00:24:28.365 --> 00:24:29.674
set it up for me and give me the context,

00:24:29.674 --> 00:24:30.905
tell me the backstory.

00:24:30.905 --> 00:24:32.912
- Yeah, it's I mean everything

00:24:32.912 --> 00:24:35.833
that you're alluding to
is mental health related.

00:24:35.833 --> 00:24:38.594
I've dealt with not only panic attacks,

00:24:38.594 --> 00:24:41.641
but anxiety and depression my entire life.

00:24:41.641 --> 00:24:44.535
It's just something that
I've always dealt with.

00:24:44.535 --> 00:24:48.433
I suffered silently
until I was 29 years old.

00:24:48.433 --> 00:24:49.803
I had an in-game panic attack

00:24:49.803 --> 00:24:52.113
against the Atlanta
Hawks on November fifth.

00:24:52.113 --> 00:24:53.832
It was a scary moment.

00:24:53.832 --> 00:24:58.832
A lot had been going just
devastatingly wrong in my life

00:24:58.934 --> 00:25:01.412
and that last pillar
to fall was basketball.

00:25:01.412 --> 00:25:02.893
- [Gary] That was always your safe place.

00:25:02.893 --> 00:25:04.092
- It was always my safe place.

00:25:04.092 --> 00:25:05.302
- [Gary] Makes sense, makes sense.

00:25:05.302 --> 00:25:06.135
- It's escapism.

00:25:06.135 --> 00:25:07.183
Everybody's looking for their escape.

00:25:07.183 --> 00:25:08.093
- [Gary] My work is mine too.

00:25:08.093 --> 00:25:10.884
- Exactly, you work and for some people

00:25:10.884 --> 00:25:12.572
it can be whatever their vice is.

00:25:12.572 --> 00:25:13.984
It could be drugs and alcohol,

00:25:13.984 --> 00:25:16.753
it can be exhausting their
body like Robin Williams,

00:25:16.753 --> 00:25:19.013
that was it for him.

00:25:19.013 --> 00:25:20.842
With Kate Spade, who
ended up taking her life,

00:25:20.842 --> 00:25:22.175
it was her work.

00:25:23.503 --> 00:25:25.954
It was everything that she
stood for and her image

00:25:25.954 --> 00:25:28.412
and that was a big
reason that she ended up

00:25:28.412 --> 00:25:31.253
not coming out and saying
what she had dealt with

00:25:31.253 --> 00:25:33.364
because she was afraid
what it was gonna do

00:25:33.364 --> 00:25:35.077
for her brand and her image.

00:25:35.077 --> 00:25:36.585
So I had an in-game panic attack.

00:25:36.585 --> 00:25:38.527
It was a really scary moment for me.

00:25:38.527 --> 00:25:40.836
I've talked at length
about it where I actually

00:25:40.836 --> 00:25:42.698
thought I was having cardiac arrest

00:25:42.698 --> 00:25:45.516
and I was looking for
something that wasn't there.

00:25:45.516 --> 00:25:46.686
I'd always had places to go,

00:25:46.686 --> 00:25:48.126
like I wasn't kidding about the rage fits,

00:25:48.126 --> 00:25:50.166
like I know I said
testosterone and all that shit

00:25:50.166 --> 00:25:52.676
when I'm done playing,
but when I was young

00:25:52.676 --> 00:25:53.957
that's how it manifested

00:25:53.957 --> 00:25:56.186
because I didn't really
know how I was feeling.

00:25:56.186 --> 00:25:58.987
I knew what the depression was more so,

00:25:58.987 --> 00:26:00.737
like I wouldn't come out
of bed for two weeks.

00:26:00.737 --> 00:26:02.757
I would play my basketball
and I would go into my room

00:26:02.757 --> 00:26:04.633
and like my brother would say,

00:26:04.633 --> 00:26:06.406
it was funny a couple weeks after

00:26:06.406 --> 00:26:08.205
I had written the Players' Tribune article

00:26:08.205 --> 00:26:10.716
and discussed that everybody's
going through something,

00:26:10.716 --> 00:26:12.597
he was like, "Oh yeah, we would just

00:26:12.597 --> 00:26:15.319
"wait for you to be Kevin again."

00:26:15.319 --> 00:26:17.037
- [Gary] But straight up two weeks?

00:26:17.037 --> 00:26:20.870
- Oh yeah, no I had
moments where it had been,

00:26:21.711 --> 00:26:23.388
there's little triggers here and there

00:26:23.388 --> 00:26:25.045
where I've developed a new relationship

00:26:25.045 --> 00:26:28.535
with both my anxiety and depression,

00:26:28.535 --> 00:26:30.875
but it could last months at a time.

00:26:30.875 --> 00:26:33.263
It could last months at a
time, so I dealt with that.

00:26:33.263 --> 00:26:35.063
- That's one thing if it's overlaying,

00:26:35.063 --> 00:26:36.557
it's another thing if
you're like physically

00:26:36.557 --> 00:26:38.266
locking yourself in a room for two weeks.

00:26:38.266 --> 00:26:40.064
- [Kevin] Yeah, 100%,
so I would go in there--

00:26:40.064 --> 00:26:42.246
- I think everybody
here is walking on earth

00:26:42.246 --> 00:26:45.395
with pressures, anxieties,
concerns, doubts.

00:26:45.395 --> 00:26:46.695
- And you find ways to hide it,

00:26:46.695 --> 00:26:48.219
like my best friends didn't even know.

00:26:48.219 --> 00:26:49.416
I would hide--

00:26:49.416 --> 00:26:51.262
- But how do they not
know Kevin a two week,

00:26:51.262 --> 00:26:53.943
like that's where I'm actually going.

00:26:53.943 --> 00:26:56.024
Like how would a best friend not know

00:26:56.024 --> 00:26:57.171
that you're locked up in two weeks

00:26:57.171 --> 00:26:59.574
or are you saying every moment I had

00:26:59.574 --> 00:27:01.623
that I didn't have to
go to school or practice

00:27:01.623 --> 00:27:03.053
I just run to the room.

00:27:03.053 --> 00:27:04.771
Or are you saying two weeks, I'm out

00:27:04.771 --> 00:27:06.482
and like I may pretend I was sick?

00:27:06.482 --> 00:27:07.704
- Basically.

00:27:07.704 --> 00:27:12.326
I mean there were times where
I either wouldn't go to school

00:27:12.326 --> 00:27:15.114
or in class I would
just be like super quiet

00:27:15.114 --> 00:27:16.722
