- [Kevin] Yeah, he's really, really good

00:45:58.308 --> 00:46:00.836
and oh my god, can you imagine what,

00:46:00.836 --> 00:46:04.870
between PJ Tucker, Russ, and James Harden,

00:46:04.870 --> 00:46:06.029
what that's gonna be like?

00:46:06.029 --> 00:46:08.214
- [Gary] It's gonna be amazing, Gav?

00:46:08.214 --> 00:46:10.214
- [Gavin] Hey Gary Vee,
how's it going guys?

00:46:10.214 --> 00:46:12.552
- Good man, say what's up to K Love.

00:46:12.552 --> 00:46:13.768
- [Gavin] Hey Kev.

00:46:13.768 --> 00:46:15.858
- What's good buddy?

00:46:15.858 --> 00:46:19.007
- [Gavin] Hey, actually my question is

00:46:19.007 --> 00:46:22.775
I heard you talking
about beating depression

00:46:22.775 --> 00:46:25.020
and having anxiety attacks and stuff,

00:46:25.020 --> 00:46:27.690
so my question to you
is how to stay strong

00:46:27.690 --> 00:46:31.039
and like stay focused on your goals

00:46:31.039 --> 00:46:33.161
while you going through that?

00:46:33.161 --> 00:46:35.422
- Yeah, I mean that's super,

00:46:35.422 --> 00:46:37.458
I mean that's probably the toughest part

00:46:37.458 --> 00:46:39.082
because a lot of the time,

00:46:39.082 --> 00:46:42.578
I mean for me it was
basically having blinders on

00:46:42.578 --> 00:46:46.259
and not seeing the effect I
was having not only on myself

00:46:46.259 --> 00:46:48.832
but other people and I also
read like a cheap novel

00:46:48.832 --> 00:46:51.621
like everybody knows when
I'm really going through it.

00:46:51.621 --> 00:46:56.131
But I always say, you know for
me it was chasing basketball,

00:46:56.131 --> 00:46:58.459
it was chasing my vice, it
was chasing what I love,

00:46:58.459 --> 00:47:00.209
and I always feel like if
you chase what you love

00:47:00.209 --> 00:47:02.090
everything you love will
chase you right back,

00:47:02.090 --> 00:47:04.691
everything you want out of this life,

00:47:04.691 --> 00:47:08.490
but you know you have to
find ways to manage it,

00:47:08.490 --> 00:47:11.631
especially you know at your worst times.

00:47:11.631 --> 00:47:14.112
And a lot of people
can't afford a therapist,

00:47:14.112 --> 00:47:18.260
they can't afford going to see
someone and paying for that,

00:47:18.260 --> 00:47:20.533
so you have to find tools
whether it be meditation,

00:47:20.533 --> 00:47:25.194
whether it be exercising,
getting that endorphin release,

00:47:25.194 --> 00:47:27.838
finding ways to deal with
your serotonin levels,

00:47:27.838 --> 00:47:29.830
whether it be medication.

00:47:29.830 --> 00:47:31.494
- I've noticed a lot of kids
hitting me up about eating,

00:47:31.494 --> 00:47:33.485
like eating behaviors changed a lot.

00:47:33.485 --> 00:47:35.964
- By the way, eating changes everything.

00:47:35.964 --> 00:47:36.994
I just read it--

00:47:36.994 --> 00:47:38.756
- Sunan wants me to like, him and his dad,

00:47:38.756 --> 00:47:40.885
who's like some crazy Turkish like genius,

00:47:40.885 --> 00:47:42.359
we're about to, like I'm
about to go there Sunan.

00:47:42.359 --> 00:47:44.531
I told you in the back
of the car, I'm ready.

00:47:44.531 --> 00:47:46.371
Take all my blood, like I'm ready.

00:47:46.371 --> 00:47:47.912
I'm about to fucking change that shit up.

00:47:47.912 --> 00:47:49.131
- But you can change your life,

00:47:49.131 --> 00:47:50.670
like your relationship with your body

00:47:50.670 --> 00:47:53.941
I feel like is, is so, so key.

00:47:53.941 --> 00:47:57.400
I mean I know that, but
I've lost so much weight.

00:47:57.400 --> 00:47:58.889
I've kept the inflammation
down in my body.

00:47:58.889 --> 00:48:00.550
I think chronic inflammation,

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:01.592
like I've seen it in my father,

00:48:01.592 --> 00:48:02.802
I've seen in a ton of people.

00:48:02.802 --> 00:48:03.690
I've seen it in retired athletes.

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:05.480
I've seen it in, we've
mentioned football players,

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:06.651
whether they're retired or not.

00:48:06.651 --> 00:48:09.112
Like when you feel like
shit getting out of bed

00:48:09.112 --> 00:48:09.945
every day in the morning,

00:48:09.945 --> 00:48:11.010
you're like this day's gonna suck.

00:48:11.010 --> 00:48:12.630
This day's gonna be terrible.

00:48:12.630 --> 00:48:14.952
- Pain's bad, mental or physical.

00:48:14.952 --> 00:48:19.048
- Exactly, but you know
I would just say yeah,

00:48:19.048 --> 00:48:22.092
finding different ways to manage it

00:48:22.092 --> 00:48:24.240
and finding different ways
to change the relationship

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:26.550
with whether it be your
anxiety or depression

00:48:26.550 --> 00:48:29.090
or any phase of mental illness.

00:48:29.090 --> 00:48:31.824
I'm a type of person that
medication has worked for me.

00:48:31.824 --> 00:48:32.930
- [Gary] Has?

00:48:32.930 --> 00:48:33.763
- Yes.

00:48:33.763 --> 00:48:34.596
- [Gary] Keep going.

00:48:34.596 --> 00:48:35.949
- You know cognitive behavioral therapy

00:48:35.949 --> 00:48:38.360
has worked for me as well

00:48:38.360 --> 00:48:41.550
and science and data has showed that

00:48:41.550 --> 00:48:42.949
that can actually prove to be

00:48:42.949 --> 00:48:45.837
more beneficial over time than meds.

00:48:45.837 --> 00:48:48.392
But getting back to the
therapist part for a sec,

00:48:48.392 --> 00:48:53.053
this can be a friend, this can
be listening to your podcast,

00:48:53.053 --> 00:48:54.693
finding ways to deal with other shit,

00:48:54.693 --> 00:48:58.573
deal with business, deal with
anything that you're into.

00:48:58.573 --> 00:49:00.022
It could be a family member.

00:49:00.022 --> 00:49:02.762
It can be somebody who, you know,

00:49:02.762 --> 00:49:06.132
a parent of one of your kids friends,

00:49:06.132 --> 00:49:08.471
it can be anybody within arm's distance.

00:49:08.471 --> 00:49:10.098
- This is a place where
I tread very lightly

00:49:10.098 --> 00:49:11.611
'cause I like talking about shit I know

00:49:11.611 --> 00:49:14.351
and so take it for what it's worth Gavin.

00:49:14.351 --> 00:49:18.298
I think one thing that I've
seen a ton of messages from

00:49:18.298 --> 00:49:20.351
is finding somebody who's
a little bit further away

00:49:20.351 --> 00:49:23.780
from your inner circle so it
doesn't affect that dynamic,

00:49:23.780 --> 00:49:26.538
has been really fascinating
how people have pinged me that.

00:49:26.538 --> 00:49:27.580
- [Kevin] I never thought of that before.

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:30.228
- You know like when it's
your mom or your brother

00:49:30.228 --> 00:49:33.299
over time that can create
something within that dynamic

00:49:33.299 --> 00:49:34.971
and having the luxury
of it being a little,

00:49:34.971 --> 00:49:36.438
you know if you think about a therapist--

00:49:36.438 --> 00:49:37.629
- [Kevin] A step removed.

00:49:37.629 --> 00:49:39.087
- Or three steps removed.

00:49:39.087 --> 00:49:40.811
Like friend a friend or acquaintance.

00:49:40.811 --> 00:49:42.079
It's been really interesting.

00:49:42.079 --> 00:49:43.490
- And maybe that person will,

00:49:43.490 --> 00:49:45.287
it comes from an unbiased point of view.

00:49:45.287 --> 00:49:46.389
It's not gonna ruin your relationship.

00:49:46.389 --> 00:49:47.721
- It's not weighted right,
you're talking to your mom,

00:49:47.721 --> 00:49:49.109
she's thinking about your dad.

00:49:49.109 --> 00:49:50.859
There's just so many other variables.

00:49:50.859 --> 00:49:55.279
That's why the therapist's so
far removed has such value.

00:49:55.279 --> 00:49:57.120
- [Kevin] They'll probably catch something

00:49:57.120 --> 00:49:58.150
that you haven't seen.

00:49:58.150 --> 00:50:00.906
- Back to your point, not
being able to afford it.

00:50:00.906 --> 00:50:02.608
One kid wrote me this manifest,

00:50:02.608 --> 00:50:03.819
I love reading my comments.

00:50:03.819 --> 00:50:05.087
It's how I learned so much insights

00:50:05.087 --> 00:50:06.620
that I'm not going through.

00:50:06.620 --> 00:50:09.047
This kid talked about
something of me talking about

00:50:09.047 --> 00:50:11.247
like not over judging yourself,

00:50:11.247 --> 00:50:12.631
it led him to another thing,

00:50:12.631 --> 00:50:13.980
then he read something
about something else.

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:17.071
What it led to was it being
a teacher in his school

00:50:17.071 --> 00:50:20.711
that he doesn't even have as a teacher.

00:50:20.711 --> 00:50:22.760
He found it as a removed thing.

00:50:22.760 --> 00:50:24.149
like it really worked for him

00:50:24.149 --> 00:50:25.442
and I was like ooh, that's,

00:50:25.442 --> 00:50:27.282
you know when you think
about the DNA of a teacher

00:50:27.282 --> 00:50:28.921
they want to help, a lot of them.

00:50:28.921 --> 00:50:30.272
- My counselor in high school,

00:50:30.272 --> 00:50:32.330
when I told her that I wanted to go

00:50:32.330 --> 00:50:34.852
to North Carolina or UCLA, these places,

00:50:34.852 --> 00:50:37.760
she was like, "Your GPA doesn't indicate",

00:50:37.760 --> 00:50:38.789
I go no, no, no.

00:50:38.789 --> 00:50:41.072
I'm gonna go to UCLA then
I'm gonna go do this.

00:50:41.072 --> 00:50:43.731
- We're you like hey, I
drop 30 a fucking game.

00:50:43.731 --> 00:50:44.872
Do you understand how this actually works?

00:50:44.872 --> 00:50:46.082
- Yeah, exactly.

00:50:46.082 --> 00:50:47.742
But I was just like you know to the point

00:50:47.742 --> 00:50:50.180
like there's you know, we
talked about the the parenting

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:52.923
and some of them are just fucking

00:50:52.923 --> 00:50:54.502
setting their kids up for failure.

00:50:54.502 --> 00:50:56.901
- It's like practical optimism is amazing

00:50:56.901 --> 00:50:58.300
and everything else sucks.

00:50:58.300 --> 00:51:00.860
Pessimism sucks, delusion sucks.

00:51:00.860 --> 00:51:01.822
- But instead of just saying that,

00:51:01.822 --> 00:51:03.532
why don't you say okay,
now show me the tool,

00:51:03.532 --> 00:51:06.221
show me the way in order
to get to that point.

00:51:06.221 --> 00:51:07.981
- Gav, do you beat yourself up?

00:51:07.981 --> 00:51:11.392
Like if I asked you that
question, how do you go with that?

00:51:11.392 --> 00:51:12.692
- [Gavin] I'm sorry, what was that again?

00:51:12.692 --> 00:51:15.391
- I said if I ask you do
you beat yourself up, right?

00:51:15.391 --> 00:51:16.851
Like something happens and you're the one

00:51:16.851 --> 00:51:20.552
beating yourself up, is that
like part of your framework?

00:51:20.552 --> 00:51:22.523
- [Gavin] Yes, I definitely
do that I would say,

00:51:22.523 --> 00:51:24.682
like just sometime I just get really,

00:51:24.682 --> 00:51:26.731
I definitely beat myself up for sure.

00:51:26.731 --> 00:51:30.092
- I've just been spending so
much time like with myself,

00:51:30.092 --> 00:51:32.734
it's why nobody's really
seen any content about it

00:51:32.734 --> 00:51:35.233
'cause again because of
what's going on with me

00:51:35.233 --> 00:51:37.210
I want to be very careful
to stay in my lane

00:51:37.210 --> 00:51:39.521
and not overstep, but it's just you know?

00:51:39.521 --> 00:51:41.894
I got nothing else to say other than man,

00:51:41.894 --> 00:51:42.953
I just want to say one thing,

00:51:42.953 --> 00:51:43.934
I got so much love for you.

00:51:43.934 --> 00:51:46.933
Please understand that the
voice that beats yourself up,

00:51:46.933 --> 00:51:49.185
I genuinely believe this.

00:51:49.185 --> 00:51:50.734
I really don't think it's your voice.

00:51:50.734 --> 00:51:52.485
I think it's a voice that's been input

00:51:52.485 --> 00:51:53.835
and I think once you understand that,

00:51:53.835 --> 00:51:55.765
it liberates you when you start realizing

00:51:55.765 --> 00:51:57.814
you're not the person booing yourself.

00:51:57.814 --> 00:51:59.335
And if you can figure that part out,

00:51:59.335 --> 00:52:01.183
if that could start the part of a thesis

00:52:01.183 --> 00:52:02.684
or something that you discover

00:52:02.684 --> 00:52:04.075
I think it could be liberating in a way

00:52:04.075 --> 00:52:06.405
that you can't believe what
that will do for you bro.

00:52:06.405 --> 00:52:08.232
- Yeah and to know that
you're not alone too,

00:52:08.232 --> 00:52:10.503
like I think it's predisposition,

00:52:10.503 --> 00:52:13.534
like we're all taught early
on when we start speaking

00:52:13.534 --> 00:52:17.136
and start absorbing what
our parents are saying

00:52:17.136 --> 00:52:19.014
or the people around us are saying,

00:52:19.014 --> 00:52:20.764
we're kind of taught how to think

00:52:20.764 --> 00:52:22.854
and that voice in your
head, that narrative,

00:52:22.854 --> 00:52:25.361
that me, can really fuck you up.

00:52:25.361 --> 00:52:26.436
- [Gary] Real quick.

00:52:26.436 --> 00:52:29.033
- Really quick, so until
you start doing that work

00:52:29.033 --> 00:52:31.466
and then continue because you can have,

00:52:31.466 --> 00:52:33.613
it's like I'll relate it to basketball.

00:52:33.613 --> 00:52:35.561
You can have a great game

00:52:35.561 --> 00:52:39.291
and that feeling only
lasts for 24 to 48 hours,

00:52:39.291 --> 00:52:42.304
whereas you have a terrible
game, it's a national TV game,

00:52:42.304 --> 00:52:43.912
and you feel like you're
set up for failure.

00:52:43.912 --> 00:52:46.252
That could last for two weeks.

00:52:46.252 --> 00:52:48.021
That's how negative thoughts are.

00:52:48.021 --> 00:52:49.464
- And I'm also trying to
think about how to turn

00:52:49.464 --> 00:52:51.423
that into positive because
like one of the things

00:52:51.423 --> 00:52:53.249
that's really interesting about me is

00:52:53.249 --> 00:52:55.501
I do not smell roses.

00:52:55.501 --> 00:52:58.682
So like back to the
Yankees and the Rangers,

00:52:58.682 --> 00:53:01.581
I dropped them the second
after the championship.

00:53:01.581 --> 00:53:04.362
You know like I'm trying to
think about why I talk about

00:53:04.362 --> 00:53:07.808
process a lot of times
is like to your point,

00:53:07.808 --> 00:53:10.681
the national televised game like,

00:53:10.681 --> 00:53:13.072
it's not that I dwell about it.

00:53:13.072 --> 00:53:13.905
I don't know.

00:53:13.905 --> 00:53:15.101
There's something I've been
thinking a lot about lately,

00:53:15.101 --> 00:53:17.142
which is like with everything
that's going on with me,

00:53:17.142 --> 00:53:19.130
I talk a lot about not hearing the boos,

00:53:19.130 --> 00:53:20.462
I don't give a fuck what you think,

00:53:20.462 --> 00:53:21.550
but it goes both ways.

00:53:21.550 --> 00:53:23.851
I also don't hear the accolades.

00:53:23.851 --> 00:53:27.222
Like so many people as my
notoriety and awareness grows

00:53:27.222 --> 00:53:28.371
and things that are going on with me,

00:53:28.371 --> 00:53:29.602
they're always like man you're so humble

00:53:29.602 --> 00:53:31.371
and every time I hear somebody say that

00:53:31.371 --> 00:53:33.663
I say it's because I can't hear your boos.

00:53:33.663 --> 00:53:35.443
That is literally what
happens when somebody,

00:53:35.443 --> 00:53:36.582
you know 'cause they don't expect it,

00:53:36.582 --> 00:53:39.338
especially the way I am
on video and on stage,

00:53:39.338 --> 00:53:41.621
they get confused by like the humility

00:53:41.621 --> 00:53:42.942
when they actually get to know me.

00:53:42.942 --> 00:53:45.619
And it's so funny how intuitive I feel

00:53:45.619 --> 00:53:47.452
that's completely laddered to me

00:53:47.452 --> 00:53:49.673
not hearing people that shit on me.

00:53:49.673 --> 00:53:51.681
Like I don't get high when they cheer

00:53:51.681 --> 00:53:52.950
and I don't get like low--

00:53:52.950 --> 00:53:54.671
- We say in the NBA fail quickly,

00:53:54.671 --> 00:53:57.721
but it's also having
success quickly as well.

00:53:57.721 --> 00:53:58.641
Like as a human--

00:53:58.641 --> 00:54:00.011
- I appreciate it Gav,
I wish you well bro.

00:54:00.011 --> 00:54:01.111
Really, thanks for calling.

00:54:01.111 --> 00:54:02.420
- [Gavin] Awesome, thank you guys.

00:54:02.420 --> 00:54:03.300
Thank you have a good one.

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:06.243
- Kev, one of my favorite
things in the world of sports

00:54:06.243 --> 00:54:10.271
that maps to life is when the
home team boos the home team

00:54:10.271 --> 00:54:12.753
after the road team went on a 11-0 run

00:54:12.753 --> 00:54:14.590
and you call a timeout.

00:54:14.590 --> 00:54:16.431
And I've been thinking
about this for two years,

00:54:16.431 --> 00:54:18.962
I don't know what it is
about that singular moment

00:54:18.962 --> 00:54:20.483
in all the sports I watch.

00:54:20.483 --> 00:54:21.911
Like you know I love boxing,

00:54:21.911 --> 00:54:23.501
one of my favorite fighters
of all time just passed away,

00:54:23.501 --> 00:54:25.949
got hit by a car, Sweet Pea Whitaker.

00:54:25.949 --> 00:54:27.841
Pernell Whitaker, I don't
know if you know that name

00:54:27.841 --> 00:54:29.629
and I loved him for defense.

00:54:29.629 --> 00:54:31.910
He was Mayweather before
Mayweather in defense

00:54:31.910 --> 00:54:33.631
and I'm a counter
puncher as a businessman,

00:54:33.631 --> 00:54:35.381
so I associate with him and--

00:54:35.381 --> 00:54:37.079
- I would be like the Tyson Fury.

00:54:37.079 --> 00:54:39.278
- I get it, he was so charismatic.

00:54:39.278 --> 00:54:40.156
- [Kevin] He's the best.

00:54:40.156 --> 00:54:40.989
- The best.

00:54:40.989 --> 00:54:41.900
- [Kevin] And he has a great story too.

00:54:41.900 --> 00:54:43.360
He should be on here if
he hasn't been already.

00:54:43.360 --> 00:54:44.579
- I'd like to get him on here.

00:54:44.579 --> 00:54:47.090
Tyson if you're listening, hit me up.

00:54:47.090 --> 00:54:50.037
I don't know what it
is about the home team

00:54:50.037 --> 00:54:54.349
letting the road team go
on a 11-0 run, timeout,

00:54:54.349 --> 00:54:55.479
and the whole fucking place,

00:54:55.479 --> 00:54:57.249
everywhere, even the best teams.

00:54:57.249 --> 00:54:59.440
By the way, ironically
back to a meta story.

00:54:59.440 --> 00:55:02.118
I went to the Knicks-Cavs opener

00:55:02.118 --> 00:55:03.437
when LeBron came back--

00:55:03.437 --> 00:55:04.290
- [Kevin] Ugh.

00:55:04.290 --> 00:55:06.200
- Yeah, best, I was so pumped.

00:55:06.200 --> 00:55:08.357
So being in that game right obviously--

00:55:08.357 --> 00:55:11.030
- Carmello hit that left
wing mid range, yeah.

00:55:11.030 --> 00:55:13.610
- Yes, so even like off
all the fucking accolades

00:55:13.610 --> 00:55:16.887
and Brons back in that game,
it's the first game back.

00:55:16.887 --> 00:55:20.158
Everyone's euphoria, Knicks
going like a 7-0 run,

00:55:20.158 --> 00:55:23.458
time out, boo, and I
sit there and I'm like

00:55:23.458 --> 00:55:26.247
there is something about this home.

00:55:26.247 --> 00:55:27.262
Back to how I think about life.

00:55:27.262 --> 00:55:28.395
- [Kevin] It's fragile,

00:55:28.395 --> 00:55:30.234
it's what have you done for me lately?

00:55:30.234 --> 00:55:33.534
- It also makes me want people
to think about life that way,

00:55:33.534 --> 00:55:35.822
which is like you could
have a shitty three years

00:55:35.822 --> 00:55:37.963
and it's still just a second quarter,

00:55:37.963 --> 00:55:40.184
life just went on a 7-0 run on you.

00:55:40.184 --> 00:55:44.056
Like what's the score at triple zero?

00:55:44.056 --> 00:55:47.286
I don't know, I mean Bulls
who I hate with all my heart,

